From 2bf952ab816d2ab472aec6265daf4d9752b7fccb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 12:00:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 65/66] dynarmic: one page table walk for loads and stores that
 share a base (A32, page table without fastmem)

Without fastmem every guest load and store walks the page table inline,
about 13 instructions. A32 code does many through one base: a function's
PUSH {r4-r11, lr} and VPUSH {d8-d15} are 17 stores, their POPs 17 loads,
each with its own walk. On the host, with the PS4's configuration, a call
with such a prologue and epilogue cost 71-124 ns, the same without them 6.

- A32HostPointer(start, span, is_write) walks once for [start, start+span):
  the host address of start, or 0 when the span crosses into the next
  page, the page is unmapped, or it's marked and a write is among them.
- A32ReadMemoryVia{8,16,32,64} and A32WriteMemoryVia{8,16,32,64} use it:
  test, branch, one host load or store. Their slow path (out of line) is
  the access's own walk, with two registers saved on the host stack, and
  only if that fails the memory callbacks; its address is the start plus
  its offset, so the fast path computes no addresses.
- The translator uses them for LDM, STM, PUSH, POP (ARM, Thumb, Thumb-2),
  VLDM, VSTM, VPUSH, VPOP and a double's VLDR and VSTR
  (A32::IREmitter::BeginMultipleAccess), and A32SharedWalkPass groups any
  other loads and stores of one base register value plus constants within
  a block (spanning at most 256 bytes).
- UserConfig::page_table_single_walk_multiple (default on) enables both,
  with a page table, without fastmem and without check_halt_on_memory_access.

Host harness (the PS4's page table configuration, shuffled host pages, a
marked and an unmapped page): calls 71-124 -> 32-42 ns, push/pop 68-86 ->
26-36 ns, a page-crossing push/pop 75 -> 66 ns, struct accesses through one
base 14-22 -> 8.4-9.6 ns; random ARM and Thumb programs at page boundaries
give the same registers and memory either way.
